WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.990
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:08.810
If you start watching reviews of some of the
more recent smartphones, you will almost always

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:11.910
see a dedicated section to low-light photography.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:17.689
The result is almost always that cameras that
work remarkably well in well-lit scenes produce

00:00:17.689 --> 00:00:20.840
almost unusable results in dim environments.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:26.109
So unless we have access to a super expensive
camera, what can we really do to obtain more

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:27.960
usable low-light images?

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.820
Well, of course, we could try brightening
the image up by increasing the exposure.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:38.300
This would help maybe a tiny bit, but would
also mess up our white balance and also amplify

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.940
the noise within the image.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:45.750
I hope that by now you are getting the feeling
that there must be a better AI-based solution.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:46.760
Let's have a look!

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.470
This is an image of a dark indoor environment,
I am sure you have noticed.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:56.080
This was taken with a relatively high light
sensitivity that can be achieved with a consumer

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.080
camera.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.560
This footage is unusable.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.140
And this image was taken by an expensive camera
with extremely high light sensitivity settings.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:09.720
This footage is kinda usable, but is quite
dim and is highly contaminated by noise.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.750
And now, hold on to your papers, because this
AI-based technique takes sensor data from

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:19.220
the first, unusable image, and produces this.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.390
Holy smokes!

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:22.000
And you know what the best part is?

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.550
It produced this output image in less than
a second.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:28.120
Let's have a look at some more results.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:33.130
These look almost too good to be true, but
luckily, we have a paper at our disposal so

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.700
we can have a look at some of the details
of the technique!

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:40.710
It reveals that we have to use a Convolutional
Neural Network to learn the concept of this

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:45.760
kind of image translation, but that also means
that we require some training data.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.710
The input should contain a bunch of dark images,
these are the before images, this can hardly

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:56.409
be a problem, but the output should always
be the corresponding image with better visibility.

00:01:56.409 --> 00:01:58.430
These are the after images.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:01:59.830
So how do we obtain them?

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:04.340
The key idea is to use different exposure
times for the input and output images.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:09.399
A short exposure time means that when taking
a photograph, the camera aperture is only

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:11.360
open for a short amount of time.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.780
This means that less light is let in, therefore
the photo will be darker.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.730
This is perfect for the input images as these
will be the ones to be improved.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:24.420
And the improved versions are going to be
the images with a much longer exposure time.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.900
This is because more light is let in, and
we'll get brighter and clearer images.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.040
This is exactly what we're looking for!

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.050
So now that we have the before and after images
that we referred to as input and output, we

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:40.959
can start training the network to learn how
to perform low-light photography well.

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:43.570
And as you see here, the results are remarkable.

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:45.730
Machine learning research at its finest.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:49.860
I really hope we get a software implementation
of something like this in the smartphones

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.650
of the near future, that would be quite amazing.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.190
And as we have only scratched the surface,
please make sure to look at the paper as it

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:57.950
contains a lot more details.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:01.430
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

